---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GuideDisclaimer from '../components/guide/GuideDisclaimer.astro';
import GuideVisual from '../components/guide/GuideVisual.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Set Your Morning Standing Wave — Return"
  description="Why practicing coherence breathing in the morning creates a lasting physiological baseline, what entrainment is, and how to feel the effect in your body."
  lang="en"
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 lg:px-8 pt-32 pb-24">
    <p class="micro-label-accent mb-4">
      <a href="/#protocol" class="hover:text-sapphire-300 transition-colors">Protocol</a>
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-light tracking-tight text-white leading-tight mb-6">
      Set Your Morning Standing Wave
    </h1>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-400 font-light leading-relaxed mb-10">
      Why practicing coherence breathing in the morning creates a lasting physiological baseline, what entrainment is, and how you can feel the effect in your body. A simple, repeatable morning anchor—without mysticism.
    </p>

    <GuideVisual kind="standing-wave" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">The Morning Practice</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The Coherence Breath is simple: 5.5 seconds inhale, 5.5 seconds exhale. That’s approximately 5.5 breaths per minute.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        If you do this breath in the morning for 5 minutes (or ideally 11 minutes), you set your baseline rhythm for the day. Your nervous system learns the pattern. It carries this rhythm forward.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Think of it like tuning an instrument before a concert. You don’t tune during the performance—you tune beforehand so the instrument produces clear sound when it matters. Morning coherence breathing is tuning and entraining your nervous system.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="pendulums" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">What is Entrainment?</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Entrainment is physics. When oscillating systems are near each other, the strongest rhythm pulls the weaker ones into sync.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Your heart, your brain, your nervous system—they all oscillate at different frequencies. When you breathe at a steady, coherent rhythm, your heart rate begins to match that rhythm. Your brainwaves follow. Your nervous system settles into the same pattern.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        It’s not mystical. It’s physics. It’s the same phenomenon that causes pendulum clocks on the same wall to synchronize their swings.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Your breath is the strongest oscillator. When you breathe coherently, you entrain everything else to that rhythm.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="body-wave" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">Where You Feel It</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        The first place you’ll feel this is in your chest. Around your heart.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-3">When coherence is established, you’ll notice:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <li>A sense of expansion or openness in the chest</li>
        <li>A calming of the flutter or tightness that might normally be there</li>
        <li>A feeling of the breath moving smoothly rather than in fits</li>
        <li>A subtle shift from “holding” to “releasing”</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        You’re generating a standing wave of stillness within yourself—a rhythm that holds steady even when the world around you is chaotic.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">The Standing Wave Metaphor</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A standing wave is a wave that appears to stay in one place. It’s created when two waves of the same frequency move in opposite directions and combine.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        When you breathe coherently, you’re creating a standing wave in your nervous system. The inhale and exhale are the two waves. When they are balanced and rhythmic, they create a stable field of coherence that remains steady even when external conditions fluctuate.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        This is why 5.5-second or other balanced breathing is so effective. It’s not just a relaxation technique. It’s literally establishing a standing wave in your physiology—a stable reference point that persists.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="sunrise" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">Why Morning Practice Matters</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Your nervous system is most impressionable in the first hour of waking. The patterns you set in that window become the baseline for the rest of the day.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        If you wake up and immediately check notifications, your nervous system locks into a reactive state. If you wake up and breathe for 5–11 minutes, your nervous system locks into coherence.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        It takes approximately 2–3 weeks of daily morning practice for this to become your new baseline. Your nervous system needs time to learn the pattern, adapt to it, and entrain to it. But once it does, coherence becomes the default state you return to throughout the day—even when you’re not consciously breathing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">The Science</h2>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Research shows that resonance frequency breathing (approximately 5.5 breaths per minute) maximizes heart rate variability (HRV)—a key marker of nervous system resilience and adaptability.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Higher HRV is associated with better stress recovery, emotional regulation, cognitive performance, and overall health. Regular practice creates lasting changes in autonomic function, shifting the baseline toward parasympathetic dominance.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-gray-500 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Source: Steffen PR, et al. “The impact of resonance frequency breathing on measures of heart rate variability, blood pressure, and mood.” Frontiers in Public Health, 2017.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideVisual kind="timer" />

    <section class="mb-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-tight text-white mb-5">The Standing Wave Practice</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed mb-5">
        <li>Inhale for 5.5 seconds</li>
        <li>Exhale for 5.5 seconds</li>
        <li>Repeat for 5–11 minutes</li>
        <li>Feel the rhythm settle in your chest</li>
        <li>Notice the standing wave of stillness you’re creating</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">This is your morning baseline. It stays with you.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-14">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-gray-300 font-light leading-relaxed">
        The world will not slow down. But you can set your internal rhythm before your day begins. That’s what the morning practice offers. A standing wave of coherence that carries through the noise.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 text-[13px] mb-12">
      <a href="/#download" class="text-signal hover:text-sapphire-400 transition-colors">Get the app →</a>
      <a href="/starting-point/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Find your starting point →</a>
    </div>

    <GuideDisclaimer />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
